<section aria-labelledby="mac">
  <h2 id="mac">Safari vs Chrome on a Mac</h2>
  <p class="prose">On a Mac the choice is less about which browser is "better" and more about which ecosystem you live in. Short version: <b>use Safari if your other devices are Apple, and Chrome if you also use Windows or Android or rely on Chrome-only extensions.</b></p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Safari</th><th scope="col">Chrome</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Battery life</th><td>Usually longer on MacBooks: Apple tunes it for its own chips and macOS power management</td><td>Shorter in most tests, especially with many tabs and video</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Memory</th><td>Lighter for the same tabs</td><td>Heavier; Memory Saver helps by unloading background tabs</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Privacy</th><td>Blocks cross-site trackers by default, hides your IP from known trackers; iCloud+ adds Private Relay</td><td>Google account and ad business at its core; tracking protection is more limited</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Extensions</th><td>Safari extensions from the App Store: the popular ones exist, but far fewer</td><td>Chrome Web Store, the largest catalogue</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sync</th><td>iPhone, iPad and Mac only, through iCloud; Handoff, Apple Pay and Passwords built in</td><td>Everywhere: Mac, Windows, Linux, Android and iPhone</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Site compatibility</th><td>Very good; the odd web app is tested on Chrome first</td><td>Best – most sites are built and tested on Chromium</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Built-in AI</th><td>Apple Intelligence summaries on supported Macs</td><td>Gemini in Chrome</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="prose">You don't have to pick only one. Many Mac users browse in Safari for battery life and keep Chrome for the few sites or extensions that need it. Both import each other's bookmarks and passwords. Want something in between? <a href="/browsers/orion">Orion</a> uses Safari's engine but runs Chrome and Firefox extensions (see <a href="/compare/orion-vs-safari">Orion vs Safari</a>), and our list of <a href="/best/browsers-for-mac">the best browsers for Mac</a> covers the rest.</p>
</section>
